1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,212
Hello, and thank you for your time.

2
00:00:03,362 --> 00:00:08,746
Today I will show you the results of our pilot project, called Wind Intelligence.

3
00:00:08,898 --> 00:00:12,615
It is a web portal, made for your day-ahead buying team.

4
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:16,059
Let me start with your problem.

5
00:00:16,209 --> 00:00:20,548
Every day, your team must decide how much power to buy for tomorrow.

6
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:23,408
Wind is a big part of Britain's power,

7
00:00:23,408 --> 00:00:27,827
and it changes every hour. So your team needs a wind forecast.

8
00:00:27,984 --> 00:00:30,771
But new forecasts come out many times a day.

9
00:00:30,771 --> 00:00:35,079
Which one could your team really use, at the moment of the decision?

10
00:00:35,234 --> 00:00:38,301
And how good are these forecasts? Can you trust them?

11
00:00:39,904 --> 00:00:45,523
In this pilot, we answered both questions, using real public data from Elexon.

12
00:00:45,674 --> 00:00:52,824
We checked 768 hours, over 32 days, against 280 forecast runs.

13
00:00:52,981 --> 00:00:58,398
Just two data sets were used, for decisions made in January 2026.

14
00:01:02,374 --> 00:01:05,454
Here is the portal. This is the Decision view.

15
00:01:05,603 --> 00:01:10,670
Your team picks the decision time. The default is 09:20, UK time,

16
00:01:10,670 --> 00:01:15,270
on the day before. That is when a day-ahead auction closes.

17
00:01:15,426 --> 00:01:20,869
Then they pick the day they are buying for. Let's take Monday, the 12th of January.

18
00:01:21,023 --> 00:01:25,959
The big number is the forecast that your team would have seen: 19.

19
00:01:25,959 --> 00:01:31,642
3 gigawatts, on average. It only uses what was published by 09:20 on Sunday.

20
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:38,200
Now look at this tile: wind to count on. It says: plan with 15 gigawatts, not 19.

21
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:44,680
In similar past hours, wind came in at or above this level, nine times out of ten.

22
00:01:44,839 --> 00:01:49,922
The row below shows what really happened. Wind averaged 14.

23
00:01:49,922 --> 00:01:54,746
4 gigawatts. The forecast was too high, in all 24 hours.

24
00:01:54,902 --> 00:01:59,820
This was one of the worst days of the month, so it shows the problem clearly.

25
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:04,802
Even the safe number was a bit high, but it was much closer than the forecast.

26
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:10,482
The chart tells the same story. Blue is the forecast.

27
00:02:10,482 --> 00:02:14,915
Orange is the real wind. The pink area is a normal range.

28
00:02:15,071 --> 00:02:18,417
Hover over any hour, and you see the exact numbers.

29
00:02:19,955 --> 00:02:24,195
Every chart also has a table view, for people who like numbers.

30
00:02:24,401 --> 00:02:29,375
Your team can also see how the forecast moved during the day, run by run.

31
00:02:29,927 --> 00:02:36,695
If the team decides at 09:50 instead, the portal picks a newer forecast, and everything updates.

32
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:41,631
Now the second question: how good is the forecast?

33
00:02:41,782 --> 00:02:46,184
Over the whole month, the forecast was too high by 1.

34
00:02:46,184 --> 00:02:50,835
8 gigawatts on average. That is 16% of the average wind.

35
00:02:50,992 --> 00:02:56,696
It was too high in 85% of the hours, and on 28 of 32 days.

36
00:02:56,847 --> 00:03:01,134
Red bars mean the forecast was too high. Most bars are red.

37
00:03:02,334 --> 00:03:06,298
The gap is biggest when the forecast is big. At 17.

38
00:03:06,298 --> 00:03:10,806
8 gigawatts or more, it was too high in every single hour.

39
00:03:10,956 --> 00:03:14,065
The forecast still beats a simple guess, like:

40
00:03:14,065 --> 00:03:18,324
tomorrow looks like now. Its typical miss is about half as big.

41
00:03:18,477 --> 00:03:23,078
So the forecast is useful, but it leans high. On this month's data,

42
00:03:23,078 --> 00:03:28,228
fixing just the average gap would shrink the typical miss by about a third.

43
00:03:29,789 --> 00:03:34,565
The Method page explains every rule in plain words, and lists the limits.

44
00:03:34,718 --> 00:03:40,501
This is one winter month, from one source. The portal has no demand or price data,

45
00:03:40,501 --> 00:03:44,591
so it says how much wind to count on, not how much to buy.

46
00:03:46,217 --> 00:03:50,424
In short, the portal shows the newest forecast your team could use,

47
00:03:50,424 --> 00:03:54,632
shows how wrong it has been, and gives a safer number to plan with.

48
00:03:54,786 --> 00:03:59,400
Good next steps are more months of data, then demand and price data,

49
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:02,385
and finding out why the forecast leans high.

50
00:04:02,541 --> 00:04:05,439
Now, part two is for the technical team.

51
00:04:08,490 --> 00:04:10,531
Part two is for the tech lead.

52
00:04:10,679 --> 00:04:14,425
I will cover the architecture, the design, the analysis,

53
00:04:14,425 --> 00:04:17,703
the code, and how we work. I will keep it simple.

54
00:04:19,312 --> 00:04:21,145
Here is the architecture.

55
00:04:21,298 --> 00:04:25,934
The front end is React with TypeScript. It shows the pages you saw.

56
00:04:26,086 --> 00:04:29,753
It calls a Python back end, built with FastAPI.

57
00:04:29,753 --> 00:04:34,902
There is no database. In production, only two packages are listed.

58
00:04:35,059 --> 00:04:40,702
The back end reads one data file: a saved copy of January from Elexon.

59
00:04:40,702 --> 00:04:47,070
January is in the past, so a file is fast, and it works even if Elexon is down.

60
00:04:47,230 --> 00:04:53,142
A script called ingest can rebuild that file. It stops with an error if there is a gap,

61
00:04:53,142 --> 00:04:56,948
a conflicting duplicate, or a row it would have to drop.

62
00:04:58,559 --> 00:05:00,546
This is the key design rule.

63
00:05:00,698 --> 00:05:04,609
Each day, Elexon publishes about eight forecast runs.

64
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:11,859
For each hour of the delivery day, we take the newest run published at or before the decision time.

65
00:05:11,859 --> 00:05:15,659
The 09:30 run is ten minutes late, so it is not used.

66
00:05:15,819 --> 00:05:19,369
This is the real function. It finds the latest run,

67
00:05:19,369 --> 00:05:23,266
at or before the cutoff, that has a value for that hour.

68
00:05:23,420 --> 00:05:27,889
Error is forecast minus actual. Positive means too high.

69
00:05:27,889 --> 00:05:31,481
Forecasts are hourly and wind is half-hourly,

70
00:05:31,481 --> 00:05:37,227
so we match start times. Other choices change the error by less than 2%.

71
00:05:38,858 --> 00:05:40,430
Now, the analysis.

72
00:05:40,580 --> 00:05:45,309
The pink range comes from past errors. We group forecasts by size,

73
00:05:45,309 --> 00:05:51,255
keep the middle 80% of the errors in each group, and blend smoothly between groups.

74
00:05:51,412 --> 00:05:57,459
The day being viewed is never used to build its own range. As a check,

75
00:05:57,459 --> 00:06:03,766
an 80% range should hold about 80% of outcomes. In our data it holds 78%.

76
00:06:03,925 --> 00:06:08,985
We also compare with a simple guess: keep the last known wind the same.

77
00:06:08,985 --> 00:06:12,548
The forecast's typical error is about half as big.

78
00:06:12,703 --> 00:06:19,112
The main limit: it is one month, and the range uses later days, so it does not promise the future.

79
00:06:23,105 --> 00:06:30,104
The API is easy to explore. FastAPI builds this documentation page by itself.

80
00:06:30,258 --> 00:06:34,447
There are four endpoints: health, meta, decision, and accuracy.

81
00:06:36,133 --> 00:06:39,061
Health returns a fingerprint of the data file,

82
00:06:39,061 --> 00:06:42,562
so we can check that the live site uses the right data.

83
00:06:43,034 --> 00:06:48,879
The decision and accuracy endpoints take the decision time as an input.

84
00:06:48,879 --> 00:06:55,795
Bad inputs get a clear error: 404 for a day outside January, and 422 for a bad time.

85
00:06:57,449 --> 00:06:59,100
Now, the code base.

86
00:06:59,251 --> 00:07:05,312
backend/app/wind holds the Python. The scope file sets the January window.

87
00:07:05,312 --> 00:07:12,520
Elexon and ingest fetch and check the data. Analytics has the maths. Routes has the API.

88
00:07:12,680 --> 00:07:19,296
frontend/src has the views and the hand-written charts. data/elexon has the snapshot.

89
00:07:19,296 --> 00:07:25,290
The docs folder and the files at the top record every decision and every bug.

90
00:07:25,450 --> 00:07:30,420
To change the time window, start in the scope file, then run ingest again.

91
00:07:30,420 --> 00:07:36,330
To add a number, add it in analytics, then in schemas and routes, and then in the views.

92
00:07:37,949 --> 00:07:39,932
Now, our key practices.

93
00:07:40,084 --> 00:07:43,594
219 Python tests, and 19 front-end tests.

94
00:07:43,594 --> 00:07:49,244
Many recalculate the numbers from the raw data, in a separate way.

95
00:07:49,399 --> 00:07:54,325
We also broke the code on purpose, in eight ways. The tests caught all eight.

96
00:07:54,325 --> 00:07:57,908
One slipped through at first, so we added a test for it.

97
00:07:58,063 --> 00:08:02,944
19 browser checks click through the real app, including the live site.

98
00:08:02,944 --> 00:08:07,198
Versions are pinned, and decisions and bugs are written down.

99
00:08:08,812 --> 00:08:14,597
To run it, use docker compose up. The site opens on port 8080.

100
00:08:14,749 --> 00:08:18,799
In production, the React build is served as static files,

101
00:08:18,799 --> 00:08:22,138
and the Python app runs as one Vercel function.

102
00:08:22,291 --> 00:08:26,674
Two honest notes. The Vercel project is not linked to GitHub,

103
00:08:26,674 --> 00:08:32,134
so a push does not redeploy it. And there is no automatic test pipeline yet.

104
00:08:33,737 --> 00:08:37,337
To finish, the limits: one month, one data source,

105
00:08:37,337 --> 00:08:41,441
no demand or price, and the cause of the bias is unknown.

106
00:08:41,595 --> 00:08:46,172
This project was built with an AI coding assistant, Claude Code,

107
00:08:46,172 --> 00:08:50,605
from Anthropic. The voice in this video is computer generated.

108
00:08:50,761 --> 00:08:52,382
Thank you for watching.

